{% set local_problems %}
<p>Hartselle may be a small town, but our homes face the same big drainage challenges as anywhere in North Alabama. With over 55 inches of rain each year and Morgan County's notorious red clay soil, proper gutter systems aren't a luxury — they're a necessity.</p>

<p>Our town has a wonderful mix of housing: charming older homes in the historic downtown area, established family neighborhoods near Sparkman Park and the Aquatic Center, and newer construction on the outskirts toward Decatur and Falkville.</p>

<p>Because we live here, we understand Hartselle's unique character. This is a community where neighbors know each other, where reputation matters, and where people expect honest work at fair prices.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_local %}
<p>In a small town like Hartselle, reputation is everything. You can't hide behind a corporate name or disappear after the job is done. We've built our business on doing right by our neighbors.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>P.O. Box 103, Hartselle, Alabama 35640. That's our mailing address. Has been for over 20 years.</p>

<p>We're not telling you this for marketing purposes. We're telling you because it means something — both to us and, we think, to you. When a company is based in your town, accountability works differently. We can't do a bad job and disappear. We're going to run into you at the grocery store, at Friday night football games, at church on Sunday. Our kids go to Hartselle City Schools. Our name is attached to every job we do.</p>

<p>That's not pressure — it's motivation. When the work we do is visible every time we drive through our own neighborhood, quality isn't optional. It's personal.</p>

<p>After two decades of serving Morgan County, we've developed an understanding of this area that you can't get from a website. We know which neighborhoods have the worst drainage problems (the low-lying areas near Priceville Road, for instance). We know which builders cut corners on gutters in the subdivisions built in the early 2000s. We've watched homes in the downtown historic district go through multiple owners, and we've often maintained their gutters through each transition.</p>

<p>Morgan County has its own characteristics that affect drainage. The land here is a mix of gentle hills and agricultural flats — former farmland that's gradually been developed into residential areas. That farmland history matters because agricultural drainage patterns don't automatically work for houses. Water that flowed across open fields now hits rooftops, driveways, and foundations. Properties on the edges of town, where development meets farmland, often have drainage challenges that require thinking beyond the property line.</p>

<p>And then there's the small-town reality of seeing the same homes generation after generation. We've worked on houses where we installed gutters 15 years ago, maintained them for a decade, and now we're helping the next owner update the system. That continuity means we know the history of a property — what's been tried, what worked, what didn't. It's institutional knowledge that a company from out of town could never have.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Here's what we'd tell our own family about taking care of a Hartselle home — because honestly, we have told our own family this.</p>

<h4>Timing Matters Here</h4>
<p>Hartselle's location in Morgan County means our weather patterns aren't quite the same as Huntsville's or Decatur's. We're far enough from the river that we don't have the constant humidity problems, but we're still fully exposed to spring storm season and fall leaf drop.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spring (March):</strong> Before storm season kicks in, make sure gutters are clear. If April's storms hit and your gutters are clogged, that water's going somewhere bad.</li>
<li><strong>Late Fall (November):</strong> After the leaves drop but before winter rains set in. Don't wait until December — by then you're cleaning cold, wet debris instead of dry leaves.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Small-Town Living Affects Home Care</h4>
<p>In a place like Hartselle, word of mouth is everything. Which means we're going to be straight with you, even when it's not in our immediate financial interest. Here's the honest truth about a few things:</p>

<p><strong>Gutter guards aren't always necessary.</strong> If you've got a single-story home with minimal tree coverage and you don't mind climbing a ladder twice a year, guards might be more than you need. We'll sell them to you if you want them, but we're not going to pretend they're essential for every Hartselle home.</p>

<p><strong>Sometimes repairs beat replacement.</strong> If your gutters are fundamentally sound but have a few problem spots, we'll tell you. Replacing a perfectly good system just because it needs some work isn't honest — and we'd rather do a repair, have you be happy, and know you'll call us when you actually need new gutters.</p>

<p><strong>Some problems aren't gutter problems.</strong> If water is pooling near your foundation but your gutters are fine, the issue might be grading or downspout placement. We'll tell you what we see, even if the fix isn't something we do.</p>

<h4>When to Call Us vs. When to Wait</h4>
<p><strong>Call now:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Gutters overflowing during rain (especially near your foundation)</li>
<li>Visible sagging or separation from the house</li>
<li>Water stains on siding below the roofline</li>
<li>Any damage after a storm</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Can wait a bit:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Minor debris accumulation (unless rain is coming)</li>
<li>Cosmetic issues that aren't affecting function</li>
<li>Questions about future upgrades (but we're always happy to talk)</li>
</ul>

<h4>The Accountability Factor</h4>
<p>Here's what being local really means: if something goes wrong, you know where to find us. We're not a 1-800 number that routes to a call center. We're your neighbors. That accountability goes both ways — we take care of you because we're going to keep seeing you around town. It's just how things work in a place like Hartselle.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set local_spotlight %}
<p>We've watched Hartselle change over 20 years. The town we started working in isn't quite the same as the town it is today — and that's mostly a good thing.</p>

<h4>The Hartselle We Remember</h4>
<p>When we started serving this community, downtown Hartselle was quieter. The antique shops along Main Street drew weekend visitors, Depot Days was the big event of the year, and most of the residential areas were established neighborhoods where families had lived for generations. Growth was slow, steady, and organic.</p>

<h4>The Hartselle That's Growing</h4>
<p>Somewhere in the past decade, Hartselle got discovered. The word spread that Morgan County has good schools, affordable housing, and an easy commute to Huntsville and Decatur. New subdivisions started appearing on the outskirts. Young families moved in. The population has grown steadily, and with it, the demand for housing.</p>

<p>That growth has been good for the town — more activity downtown, more investment in amenities, more young energy. But it's also brought the same challenges we've seen in Madison and other booming communities: builder-grade construction, rushed development, and homes that were built fast rather than built right.</p>

<h4>What Builders Get Wrong</h4>
<p>We've worked on plenty of newer Hartselle homes, and the pattern is familiar:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Minimum-spec gutters:</strong> The cheapest 5-inch aluminum that meets code but can't handle North Alabama rainfall.</li>
<li><strong>Inadequate downspouts:</strong> Too few, too small, or dumping water too close to the foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Poor grading:</strong> Lots that were scraped level for construction but never properly graded for drainage after the house was built.</li>
<li><strong>Compacted clay:</strong> Construction equipment compresses the soil, making drainage worse than it would be naturally.</li>
</ul>

<p>These aren't problems you'll notice on day one. They show up at year three, year five, year seven — when seams start failing, when settling reveals grading issues, when the shortcuts catch up.</p>

<h4>Protecting Both Harselles</h4>
<p>We serve the old Hartselle and the new Hartselle. The historic homes near downtown that need careful work respecting their character. The established family neighborhoods where we've maintained the same houses for a decade. And the newer subdivisions where we're often the second gutter company — called in to fix what the first one (usually the builder's subcontractor) got wrong.</p>

<p>Twenty years of serving our hometown has taught us that every house is part of Hartselle's story. The 1950s ranch near Sparkman Park. The 1990s family home off Highway 31. The brand-new construction on the edge of town. They all deserve gutters that actually work — installed by people who are going to be around to stand behind their work.</p>

<p>That's what it means to be Hartselle's gutter company. Not just a contractor who serves the area, but neighbors who take care of neighbors.</p>
